Vaccines pass three phases of clinical trials — typically involving 30,000–70,000 participants — before regulatory approval.
The FDA, EMA, and WHO apply identical safety standards to vaccines as to all other medicines, including ongoing post-market surveillance.
Serious adverse events occur in fewer than 1 in 100,000 doses; the CDC's VAERS database tracks every reported reaction in real time.
The risk of severe disease from the pathogen a vaccine prevents is orders of magnitude higher than the risk from the vaccine itself.
